on the streets of limbae a city in  cameroon's south west province it's not  just fans who are taking pictures  it's a matter of days until africa's  most prominent soccer championship the  africa cup of nations and security is  tight  six cities are hosting afcon matches but  limbae and nearby boya are in the  spotlight because separatist fighters  have warned the games should not be  played there my fear and the fear of  many limb denizens is that the recent  phenomenon of  bomb blasts  which has been happening in other parts  of our code division like in bowie  can be a common phenomenon during this  afghan  period  conflict broke out in cameroon in 2016  between english-speaking separatists and  the french-speaking government  the armed groups accused the government  of discriminating against them and want  to form a breakaway state called  ambassonia  the fightings killed at least 3 000  people and forced about a million from  their homes  some rights groups say any violence  could threaten teams and spectators and  the competition should be called off  cameroon hosted the african nations  championships in january and february  2021  the governing body of african football  and cameroon's government are promising  a safe tournament  i can't reveal the measures that are  being put in place but you should know  that just as the african nations  championship took place under very good  conditions afcon will also take place  under very good conditions there's  nothing to worry about  cameroon won the right to host the  finals when its team beat egypt in 2017  ending a 15-year drought  it's already been pushed back twice once  because of concerns about cameroon's  preparations and then by the pandemic  some hope it will provide a much needed  financial boost  we do believe  it will boom our business because so  many customers who come from different  areas different towns different  countries  we meet so many  other people that will meet new friends  make new friends  others though say they won't be able to  focus on the games while some and  cameroon live in fear for their lives  alex o'brien al jazeera
 

